# What Docker sends to the daemon before the build starts. Everything excluded
# here is either rebuilt inside the image (deps, assets, the release), produced
# by a developer's machine (build output, databases, blob stores), or of no use
# to the build (docs, tests, deployment configuration).
#
# A smaller context is a faster build; a context without local state is also a
# reproducible one — `COPY lib lib` must not depend on what happens to be on the
# disk.

.dockerignore

# Version control, but not for the build: the image labels take the version and
# revision as build arguments instead.
.git
.gitignore

# Build output and dependencies: fetched and compiled inside the image.
/_build/
/deps/
/assets/node_modules/
/priv/static/assets/
/priv/static/cache_manifest.json
*.ez

# Local runtime state — these are somebody's mirror, not source. The PLTs and
# the recorded runs live under priv/, which the release would otherwise carry
# into the image.
/var/
/tmp/
/cover/
/doc/
/priv/plts/
/priv/studio_runs/
erl_crash.dump
*.log

# Not needed to build the release.
/test/
/docs/
/deploy/
/k6-load-tests/
/.elixir_ls/
/.venv/
.vscode/
.ruff_cache/
__pycache__/

# Deployment configuration describes the image; it is not built into it.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.quickstart.yml
**/fly*.toml
.github/
AGENTS.md
README.md
LICENSE

# The Rust crate's build artefacts: the image compiles its own, and copying a
# `target/` directory would be hundreds of megabytes of another machine's work.
# The crates moved into the core package; their build output is the largest
# thing in the tree and the image compiles its own.
packages/*/native/**/target
